{
  "id": "g",
  "candidate": "g",
  "heard": "Start right here. With the probabilities a model assigns to the actual tokens as it reads from left to right. Multiply those probabilities and the product shrinks with length. Eventually, ordinary floating-point arithmetic can even round it to zero. So take the negative log probability of each token instead. Average those losses across the test set, then exponentiate. That number is perplexity. There is a neat way to picture it, an effective number of choices, with a vocabulary of 100,000 tokens. Uniform guessing gives you a perplexity of 100,000. Bring it down to 10, and you pay the same average loss as a model guessing uniformly among 10 options at each step. The actual number of plausible options can change from word to word.",
